Date: Thu, 1 Apr 93 17:20:00 PST
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: Traffic guide - passthrough brake lights

I don't quite see the value of Jerry's idea of pass-through brake lights.
It seems like you'd have so many "false alarms" where the brake lights of
the car in front of you didn't mean it was slowing down, that people might
tend to ignore brake lights.

That reminds me of an annoying phenomenon I've noticed while driving in
traffic at night.  When you come to the top of a hill so that you can see
the road for some distance in front of you, people tend to hit their
brakes.  I think the reason is that people have an instinctive response
to an increase in the amount of red light in front of them, causing them
to apply their brakes.  Normally this happens when the car or cars in
front of you are slowing down, so it is a useful instinct.  But at the
top of a hill the same thing happens, as you are suddenly able to see the
brake lights of cars for a mile or more in front of you, lights which were
previously hidden by other cars.  This causes a similar increase in the
amount of red light you see, causing you to hit your brakes.

Given this instinct, I'd be afraid that Jerry's idea would slow traffic
badly, causing one person's application of brakes to snowball and slow
cars down for miles behind him.

It's good for _me_ to slow cars down behind me; it reduces the chances that
I'll get rear-ended.  But it's bad for the people behind me, and it would be
bad for me if someone in front of me does it.  This is an example of the
conflict between what is good for the individual and what is good for the
group on the roads.

(Dean compared this to an iterated prisoner's dilemma, but I don't think
that applies; the IPD requires that you know who you are playing against
each time, and remember what happened in your previous encounters.  Most
of the time on the road these requirements aren't met.  Rather, I would
think this is better analyzed in terms of Hofstadter's super-rationality
(which we had a big debate on last year).  Super-rational people behave
in a way such that if everyone behaved that way, things would work out well.
Super-rational behavior is not rational, unfortunately, and after our
discussion I can't really recommend it as a general principle.)

Hal

