Date: Thu, 1 Apr 93 12:29:40 PST
From: ghsvax!hal@uunet.UU.NET (Hal Finney)
Subject: TECH: Entropic costs of computation

Let me first make a general point about these calculations regarding heat
dissipation and calculation, then I'll talk about Derek's note.

The results that come out of the equations are lower bounds.  They are
limitations which cannot fundamentally be beaten.  Given a calculation
which starts with inputs X and produces outputs Y we can say what the
minimal energy dissipation is.

This does not mean that we actually have a design in hand to produce a
machine which can calculate at that minimal energy cost.  It does not
mean that any given design calculates at that cost.  It simply means
that we know that no design, no matter how clever, can beat this cost.

This approach is similar to other thermodynamic analyses where we
calculate theoretical maximum efficiencies.  We know these numbers
can't be beaten, but that doesn't mean that any given design achieves
them.

Looking at Derek's comments from his long message in this light:

1) Heat dissipation caused by the mechanism itself.

You don't _have_ to invoke entropy to calculate how much heat is
dissipated by a particular mechanism.  That can be done by plain old
dynamics.  Look at the friction, look at the materials, look at the
motions and forces, and calculate the heating.  This will always give
you a correct answer.

Or, if that's too hard, you can use the entropy calculation to give you
a lower bound on what the answer will turn out to be.  You can apply
it to any system or subsystem.  If that subsystem decreases its
entropy, reducing its volume of phase space, then it will dissipate
heat.

On Derek's second point:

1) Deduce what was there before by restarting the calculation.

But you can't necessarily restart the calculation unless you
remembered what all the inputs were.  A machine which transforms
inputs into outputs will not necessarily record that information.

It's the difference between out = in1 xor in2, which is irreversible,
and (out1, out2) = (in1 xor in2, in2), which is reversible.

So, Derek is mistaken in saying that it is ALWAYS possible to deduce
what was there before, since that is actually only true for logically
reversible calculations.  It's just that by saving the input you can
make any calculation logically reversible, using Derek's technique.

2) Reverse motions and recover the earlier state.

This is impossible by the 2nd law of thermodynamics.  Once the energy
becomes heat it is not useful.  Yes, if you could reverse molecular
motions you could do all sorts of nice things.  But this whole
analysis is being done in the context of the 2nd law, where once
energy becomes heat it is lost.  Any discussion of acquiring energy
from (uniform) heat is totally nonphysical.

3) Who is the "you" in "if you could deduce what had been there
before"?

What I meant is, if the calculation is such that this deduction could
be done, then theoretically a machine could be built which would not
dissipate energy in doing this calculation.  If this is not the case
(as in out1 = in1 xor in2) then you know that there _must_ be a
certain minimum level of dissipation.

If I understand the point Derek is raising, the issue is whether the
_logical_ structure of the calculation allows for this kind of
deduction.  It is a question of whether the calculation is logically
reversible.  (out1, out2) = (in1 xor in2, in1 or in2) is not logically
reversible.  Above I gave an example which was.  These considerations
allow us to put bounds on the energy dissipation of the process.
There is no issue of reversing molecular motion.  It is not at this
level.

Derek also mentions a formula from a paper by Schneider (which I
haven't read).  Again, I would interpret this formula as a lower
bound.  If you discovered a new way of interpreting the data which
indicated that _more_ information was coming out (similar to Derek's
example of measuring coin position to finer resolution than just
heads/tails) then we would raise our estimate of the actual energy
dissipated by the machine.  It wouldn't _change_ the workings of the
machine - it always would have dissipated that much energy.  It means
that as we do a more detailed analysis we come to understand better
why it dissipates as much energy as it does.

In Derek's example of a long-running program which does just a little
calculation (his example is actually logically reversible, by the way,
since it retains its input) then yes, theoretically a machine could be
built to do that calculation with an arbitrarily small amount of
dissipation.  If it were doing an irreversible calculation, then we
would know that this would be impossible.  That's what the information
measurements tell us.

As far as the macrostate/microstate distinction, this is how I would
explain it.  It is true that if you include all the detailed
information about every molecule's motion, then the total phase space
volume of a system never changes.  Even if entropy increases and
information gets thermalized and "lost", if you look at things at the
atomic level the information is not actually lost.  The phase space
representation develops little "fingers" and becomes rather "fractal"
in shape but the volume remains the same.  There was a good article on
this in Sci. Am. many years ago.

But, in the real world, information that becomes thermalized is
effectively lost.  It can do no work.  There is no magical machine to
reverse molecular motions.  Our lives would be much easier if there
were!  Raising the dead would be the tiniest of our capabilities.

So, if we do as the thermodynamicists do and group microstates into
macrostates which are macroscopically indistinguishable, we have that
entropy increases, information is lost, and phase space volume
spreads out and increases.

It is in this sense that we say that a measurement can reduce the
phase space volume of a system, because before it could have been in
more states than it is now.  It is true that at the atomic level the
volume never changes.  But blurring out the indistinguishable atomic
level differences (one thermal motion is like another if they are the
same temperature) means we do see a phase space change.

This is not an unnatural or "pernicious" way of classifying
microstates into macrostates.  It is perfectly natural based on the
way the real world works.  Heat is heat, and there is no point in
trying to distinguish all the different thermal motions.  Once we
classify them all together we see entropy appear along with all of the
other phenomena we are discussing here.

Hal Finney
