Hal Finney writes:

>(Dean compared this to an iterated prisoner's dilemma, but I don't think
>that applies; the IPD requires that you know who you are playing against
>each time, and remember what happened in your previous encounters.  Most
>of the time on the road these requirements aren't met.  Rather, I would
>think this is better analyzed in terms of Hofstadter's super-rationality
>(which we had a big debate on last year).  Super-rational people behave
>in a way such that if everyone behaved that way, things would work out well.
>Super-rational behavior is not rational, unfortunately, and after our
>discussion I can't really recommend it as a general principle.)

Actually, the iterated prisoner's dilemma does *not* require the same exact
players in each round. For example, the "Russians" and the "Americans" may
play the game in various ways, even thought the individual players change
frequently. Other "public choice" games show IPD behavior--markets, for
example--without the players be exactly the same people. (The suggestion
being that an IPD-of-sorts is occurring with "the driver" and "the other
drivers" and that IPD-type emergent behavior develops. I know when I drive
I think of the other drivers as players in a game, sort of, and I try to
"do unto others as I would have them do unto me," which is the canonical
form of tit-for-tat. Whether this behavior can best be modeled as an IPD is
another matter.)

I recall reading about how IPD experiments were done where the game was
iterated as usual, but the players were rotated at each round (that is,
everybody played the same number of rounds, but never against the same
person more than once). The results were similar to the normal version.
(I've looked in my standard game theory reference, Fudenberg and Tirole,
but can't find mention of this. I'll keep my eye out for this.)

On Keith Henson's "Traffic Guide" proposal per se, the social and economic
flaws are too numerous to mention, in my opinion. Keith asked me to look
over his paper in December, 1991 and I did so, writing a 12-page outline
form critique of the many problems I saw. I don't think it's of much
Extropian interest, but since people are beginning to nibble away at
Keith's idea, perhaps I should post it?


-Tim May
