From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: TECH: Entropic costs of computation

Hal Finney writes, in a very lucid and useful response:

< but first, a comment >

It turns out that all of my intuitions are essentially correct, which
makes me more confused than ever -- because now I don't understand
why people talk about this the way they do... but I think I'm nearing
understanding, and it's not as fun an 'aha' experience as I'd hoped.
Because, apparently:

> You don't _have_ to invoke entropy to calculate how much heat is
> dissipated by a particular mechanism.  That can be done by plain old
> dynamics.  Look at the friction, look at the materials, look at the
> motions and forces, and calculate the heating.  This will always give
> you a correct answer.

< THIS IS APPARENTLY THE IMPORTANT POINT >
OK, but then WHY would "reversible" logic help?  In our rod logic
computer, the heat dissipation is caused by the little rods smashing
into each other, right?  The way to minimize this is to minimize the
bashing, right?  How can a more complex design help?

I do understand, by the way, that these are only "theoretical" minimums
requiring actual designs to reach them -- but now it seems that the
minimums are MUCH less than I'd thought.

> But you can't necessarily restart the calculation unless you
> remembered what all the inputs were.  A machine which transforms
> inputs into outputs will not necessarily record that information.

I was assuming that saving the inputs somewhere would be relatively
trivial compared to the bazillion-year calculation.

> 2) Reverse motions and recover the earlier state.
> This is impossible by the 2nd law of thermodynamics.

Yes, but isn't josh's reversible demon impossible for the same reasons?
if it was able to make the necessary measurements and undo its work,
why can't other mechanisms do the same thing?

> In Derek's example of a long-running program which does just a little
> calculation (his example is actually logically reversible, by the way,
> since it retains its input) then yes, theoretically a machine could be
> built to do that calculation with an arbitrarily small amount of
> dissipation.

Then what's all the fuss?  Why would josh's computer he talked about
designing in Extropy #10 explode?  He bases that on looking at the
individual logic gates from an information-theoretic viewpoint.  OK,
if it turns out that this is correct (I'm still not quite convinced of
that), then what his calculation means is that the rods bouncing off
each other have to give off at minimum the calculated amount of heat.
He fixes this with baroque "reversible" mechanisms... which surely
would dissipate even more!

> So, if we do as the thermodynamicists do and group microstates into
> macrostates which are macroscopically indistinguishable, we have that
> entropy increases, information is lost, and phase space volume
> spreads out and increases.

So if I treat the internal workings of my computer as macroscopically
indistinguishable (by hiding them, say), then I don't have to worry
about theoretical minimums on the internal workings?

>From all this, it appears that the answer to the puzzle is simply
that we're going to have friction in our nanocomputer, and we should
try to minimize it.  This is hardly a profound or deep thought!
It's exactly what I'd expect!  Talk of limits due to information
theory are sort of red herrings, aren't they?

derek
