Subject: Traffic guide - passthrough brake lights

Hal,

It's true that passthrough lights have drawbacks,  such as false indications
being more annoying than with normal tail lights.  A sufficiently smart system
could figure out which indications to ignore (by looking for the "nose dip", as
some people do, or noting the d/dt of the angle subtended by the taillights),
but your point is well taken.

Re braking behavior on hilltops, my *perception* is that when the road is
concave up (in the math sense) I can relax more while at the same time driving
much more efficiently and appropriately, in no small part because one rare but
deadly possibility (car ahead of us whips to the side, revealing a stationary
car 20 feet ahead of *it*) has been removed.

Of course the road cannot be concave up always:-).  In the spirit of
brainstorming I'll point out that we would achieve the same end if we could
arrange for light rays to be slightly concave *down* over roadways  (via a
height-variable refractive index, for instance; I believe that this is the case
on the surface of Venus, i.e. that a Venusian would look up to see the
horizon), but this sounds real expensive here on earth:-)

Cheers
Jerry

